# Question: with three features competing for the same team, which one should
# go first by CD3 (cost of delay divided by duration), and how likely is the
# small export feature to be the right first pick?
#
# Run it (it uses the shared formulas):
#   ubc scenarios/formulas.ubc scenarios/product/cd3.ubc
# Change an assumption, for example a search feature that turns out smaller:
#   ubc --set 'weeks_search = 2 to 3' scenarios/formulas.ubc scenarios/product/cd3.ubc
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens cd3_export scenarios/formulas.ubc scenarios/product/cd3.ubc
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Costs of delay are in euros a
# week, durations in weeks. CD3 (Reinertsen; Joshua Arnold) does first the
# work that loses the most value per week the team spends on it, so a small
# feature with a modest cost of delay can beat a big valuable one.

# Assumptions
cod_search = 5k to 15k            # better search: weekly value lost while it is missing
weeks_search = 4 to 8             # weeks the team needs to build it
cod_export = 2k to 6k             # export to spreadsheets: asked for by many customers
weeks_export = 1 to 3
cod_billing = 8k to 30k           # usage-based billing: big value, big job
weeks_billing = 8 to 20

# Calculation
cd3_search = cd3(cod_search, weeks_search)        # value lost per week of work, search
cd3_export = cd3(cod_export, weeks_export)        # the same for the export
cd3_billing = cd3(cod_billing, weeks_billing)     # the same for billing

# Result
cd3_search
cd3_export
cd3_billing
P(cd3_export > max(cd3_search, cd3_billing))      # how likely is the export to be the right first pick?
